#{[17592186370894 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 7 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{26}{5} & \\frac{38}{5} & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} & ={x}_{3}-2(\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}) \\\\ 5{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{25}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=13$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=-\\frac{5}{19}.$"] [17592186371063 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186371714 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(-1-4),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-5,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371728 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(0) & =T(0)\\times N(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨(0-0),-(-1-1),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371941 "Notice that $x={z}^{4}+1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=4{z}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+(4{z}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186371948 "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,e].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ and\n\n$$\nD=\\{(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373278 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373428 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =⟨0-0,-(-{cos}^{2}⁡3t-{sin}^{2}⁡3t),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373435 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{5{e}^{2t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t})^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-5{e}^{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}+25{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-5{e}^{2t}-1,-(-10{e}^{2t}-2),2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,2(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373442 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,1⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\sqrt{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373965 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}2u-4v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,-1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}4,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨z+x,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-4y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=2u-4v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-4v+u,\\hspace{0.222em}v+2(2u-4v),\\hspace{0.222em}u-4v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u-4v,\\hspace{0.222em}4u-7v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-4v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3u-4v,\\hspace{0.222em}4u-7v,\\hspace{0.222em}u-4v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\hspace{0.222em}4,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-4v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-7v)\\cdot 4+(u-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-6u+8v+16u-28v+u-4v \\\\  & =11u-24v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+x,\\hspace{0.222em}y+2z,\\hspace{0.222em}x-4y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11u-24v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{\\color{blue}(11u-24v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186373986 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}4u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}4⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨2x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}z+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=4u-v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-(4u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}v-(4u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}4u-v+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}-4u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}4u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨-2u+v,\\hspace{0.222em}-4u+2v,\\hspace{0.222em}4u⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2u+v)\\cdot (-4)+(-4u+2v)\\cdot 1+4u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8u-4v+-4u+2v+4u \\\\  & =8u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x-z,\\hspace{0.222em}y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}z+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8u-2v)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\color{blue}(8u-2v)}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375846 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,3)$ is $p=⟨0,1,3⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =i\\times j \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=k=⟨0,0,1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩ & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(0)+z(1) & =0(0)+1(0)+3(1) \\\\ 0+0+z & =0+0+3 \\\\ z-3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375853 "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,2)$ is $p=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =i\\times (-k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi )=j=⟨0,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(1)+z(0) & =0(0)+1(1)+2(0) \\\\ 0+y+0 & =0+1+0 \\\\ y-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375860 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=0$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\ N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\np=r(0)=⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,2,\\,-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0,2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(0)$ and the principal normal $N(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0)\\times N(0) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(0)\\times N(0)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(-1)+z(0) & =0(0)+2(-1)+(-1)(0) \\\\ 0-y+0 & =0-2+0 \\\\ y-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375867 "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and the principal normal $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186375958 "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda 