on form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\,4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386134 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 4\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386459 "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,-2\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=(1,\\sqrt{3},0),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-2,\\,-2\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-\\sqrt{3},\\,z⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (x-1)-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (y-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ -2x-2\\sqrt{3\\,}y+8 & =0 \\\\ -x-\\sqrt{3}\\,y+4 & =0 \\\\ x+\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386466 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386473 "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨1+{e}^{u},\\,2-{e}^{v},\\,1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+{e}^{u}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{u},\\,0,\\,-{e}^{u}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+{e}^{u}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{e}^{v},\\,-{e}^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,0,\\,-{e}^{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,0,\\,-e⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨0,\\,-{e}^{-1},\\,-{e}^{-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ e & 0 & -e \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1} & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -e \\\\ -{e}^{-1} & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}e & -e \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386480 "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-v\\operatorname{sin}u⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}v & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}v & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -v\\operatorname{sin}u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}u\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v+uv\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\operatorname{cos}u,\\hspace{0.222em}u\\operatorname{cos}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386487 "First, notice that since $(2,1,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1+v &=2 \\\\ uv &=1 \\\\ {u}^{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,v,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,u,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386501 "First, notice that since $(1,1,2)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ {v}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {u}^{2}-v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨0,\\,2(-1),\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386585 "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 11 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186387817 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4t}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(1+4{t}^{2}),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,\\,-(1+4{t}^{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388716 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388723 "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -9\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+7{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 11\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388799 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388960 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388967 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388974 "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389002 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389126 "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389567 "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a horizontal shear of shear factor $1.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n  \n\n![](images/cf/cfe398e1ab7e5b4b487671772bf4b6127e8e141b25491f96c5cc32e77869dec0.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the parallelogram shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391480 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c3afc4bedb411c778773190a3b9bc1db0b376dd69592ba744e8225ec900cc45.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t \\\\ 2t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-1 \\\\ y &=2t+3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/0d/0d506993d3bfbb727dffd44ced1a588b40e2fefa86e7f2780d90277c9d0a9eed.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+1 \\\\ y &=-2t-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e8/e88dfa02c57a5c31df14fdf6f963f7d568eaebccbbbfba6d8f2958593ac4693a.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3f80cbae958772e0fa0492f5c426d412f002c3baa41a8a69b38e594512823422.png)"] [17592186391512 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-2x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/60/6044d391016e37af929b36e89e0a802151b5710707cc510e9e7f70bf9a2d1c0d.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-t-1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t-2 \\\\ y &=2t+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/54/54bdfaf14b4f580e1476d4649e03cd71cb8203e23ae318daa65251fd7ce02ba1.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t+2 \\\\ y &=-2t-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/ed/edb5558ed7b5ee0e4aacb4eb67d74747fcc366f1508e16b3e99f3e0d5ee53306.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/10/1005575ad1d73527ce1bd4e946aa9a3d0be86ae82329fde27700082ea2b5c121.png)"] [17592186391790 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+7=13.$"] [17592186392045 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0 &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186392166 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}3 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{7}{3} & -2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & \\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5} & -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186392340 "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{11}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+11\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392354 "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392361 "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392368 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={30}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -10 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392402 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186392422 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{42}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393376 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 2\\mathbf{x} & =4\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 30 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393383 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{a}+5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b} \\\\ 5\\mathbf{x} & =\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -30 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393431 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=4T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394078 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394092 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394133 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}b \\\\  & =4b \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394140 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=6{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6+5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1}{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{41}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394161 "Looking at the matrix $T$, we can see that $T$ represents a stretch in the $x$-direction of scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the image of the square $R$ under $T$ is\n\n![](images/72/72c88d1df90c3462d7f1f186c06b9a7acf30c071f5ba8bbf722ee125fee0e616.png)\n\n**Alternative method:**\n\nWe can check that the vertices of $R$ are mapped to the vertices of the rectangle shown above as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\