atrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394511 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186394518 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\